    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d797">

                <pb facs="0820" n="506" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg506"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841/731/a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-23T20:00:00-05:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 23, 1914, 8 p.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Department is informed that British censors will not pass the usual service
                    correspondence provided for in international telegraph regulations whereby
                    errors made in transmission are corrected without expense to the public, and
                    require that all such corrections shall be made by private messages exchanged
                    between addressees and senders and paid for. As this restriction apparently
                    possesses no possible military features, but bears solely upon an administrative
                    feature of international cable service and greatly increases cost to cabling
                    public, make representations to British Government looking to removal of present
                    embargo on repetition requests.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
